{
  "abstract": "A global periodic skeleton fixes every residue class except specified holes, whose fillings may vary between blocks. We prove that a non-eventually-periodic binary parity word or bounded division-exponent word with at most two holes at unbounded skeleton periods has no positive rational Collatz source with odd denominator. No nesting, separation, or density hypothesis is needed. When all internal and boundary gaps between $m$ holes grow, a quadratic auxiliary lowers the exponent-mean threshold in the contracting range from $m\\log_2(3)/(m+1)$ to $m\\log_2(3)/(m+2)$ and gives a corresponding binary density bound. Combined with the inherited shadow argument, this excludes all means or densities for non-eventually-periodic words with three separated holes. An uncountable family with means approaching one falls outside the specific prior shadow and dictionary criteria compared here.\n\nFixed-width boundary strips can contain any number of holes: a formal unit reduction gives bounds depending only on the separated interior holes and excludes one interior hole at every mean or density for non-eventually-periodic words.\n\nFor exponent words with any fixed number $m$ of holes and contracting mean, a prime-$3$ estimate on inverse tails shows that positive rational execution forces a bounded integer linear form in the period and hole positions. The form comes from an explicit finite set and may change with the period. With separated holes and $m\\ge2$, execution forces two independent bounded relations.\n\nUnder uniform interval contraction and the stated gap conditions, 149 aligned blocks force exponential growth of positive rational source height with the period. For supplied positive odd integer sources, $8m-1$ and $80m-1$ blocks give finite height bounds in terms of the one- and two-relation distances, respectively. No infinite continuation is needed. The proofs are analytic; frozen audits check finite arithmetic identities. The results constrain prescribed itineraries, not arbitrary Collatz orbits.",
